    <!-- =========================
         EXAMPLE 1 - IN
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example 1: Multiple-Row Subquery with IN
            </h2>

            <p>
                Suppose we want to find employees who work in departments
                located in Pune.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>
SELECT EmployeeName, DepartmentId
FROM Employees
WHERE DepartmentId IN
(
    SELECT DepartmentId
    FROM Departments
    WHERE Location = 'Pune'
);
            </code></pre>

            <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
                How it works
            </h5>

            <ol>

                <li>
                    The inner query finds department IDs where the
                    location is Pune.
                </li>

                <li>
                    The inner query can return multiple department IDs.
                </li>

                <li>
                    The outer query checks whether Employee DepartmentId
                    exists in those returned values.
                </li>

            </ol>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         EXAMPLE 2 - NOT IN
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example 2: Multiple-Row Subquery with NOT IN
            </h2>

            <p>
                The <strong>NOT IN</strong> operator can be used when
                we want records that do not match any value returned
                by the subquery.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>
SELECT EmployeeName, DepartmentId
FROM Employees
WHERE DepartmentId NOT IN
(
    SELECT DepartmentId
    FROM Departments
    WHERE Location = 'Pune'
);
            </code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                This query returns employees who do not belong to
                departments located in Pune.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         EXAMPLE 3 - ANY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example 3: Using ANY
            </h2>

            <p>
                The <strong>ANY</strong> operator compares a value with
                the values returned by a subquery.
            </p>

            <p>
                For example, find employees whose salary is greater than
                the salary of <strong>ANY</strong> employee in department
                101.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>
SELECT EmployeeName, Salary
FROM Employees
WHERE Salary &gt; ANY
(
    SELECT Salary
    FROM Employees
    WHERE DepartmentId = 101
);
            </code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The condition is true when the comparison is true for
                at least one value returned by the subquery.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         EXAMPLE 4 - ALL
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example 4: Using ALL
            </h2>

            <p>
                The <strong>ALL</strong> operator compares a value with
                every value returned by the subquery.
            </p>

            <p>
                Find employees whose salary is greater than the salary
                of <strong>ALL</strong> employees in department 101.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>
SELECT EmployeeName, Salary
FROM Employees
WHERE Salary &gt; ALL
(
    SELECT Salary
    FROM Employees
    WHERE DepartmentId = 101
);
            </code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The condition must be true for every value returned by
                the subquery.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         IN vs ANY vs ALL
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                IN vs ANY vs ALL
            </h2>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-primary">

                        <tr>
                            <th>Operator</th>
                            <th>Purpose</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>IN</strong></td>
                            <td>
                                Checks whether a value matches any value
                                returned by the subquery.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>ANY</strong></td>
                            <td>
                                The comparison must be true for at least
                                one value returned by the subquery.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>ALL</strong></td>
                            <td>
                                The comparison must be true for every
                                value returned by the subquery.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-warning mb-4">

        <h4 class="fw-bold">
            Important Points
        </h4>

        <ul class="mb-0">

            <li>
                A multiple-row subquery can return more than one row.
            </li>

            <li>
                The IN operator is commonly used with multiple-row
                subqueries.
            </li>

            <li>
                NOT IN can be used to exclude matching values.
            </li>

            <li>
                ANY checks whether the comparison is true for at least
                one returned value.
            </li>

            <li>
                ALL checks whether the comparison is true for every
                returned value.
            </li>

            <li>
                Using = with a subquery that returns multiple rows can
                cause an error.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-info p-4 mb-4">

        <h2 class="fw-bold">
            Summary
        </h2>

        <p class="mb-0">
            A Multiple-Row Subquery returns multiple values or rows.
            Operators such as IN, NOT IN, ANY and ALL can be used to
            work with the returned values. Multiple-row subqueries are
            useful when the result of one query needs to be compared
            with a set of values.
        </p>

    </div>


